The CICA archivist writes:

>As is a standard practice with most anonymous-ftp sites (that are
>supported with funds from an independent host institution), certain
>programs, files, or software are outside the general scope of archive
>operations, and therefore will NOT be accepted.  These files include
>(but are not limited to) any software: containing political,
>religious, racist, ethnic, or other prejudiced or discriminatory
>messages therein; that is adult or sexually-explicit in nature;

This policy statement seems to be claiming that the Indiana U.
prohibits religious material in its archives. I'm sure that it is not
true. Moreover, in spite of this policy, the CICA archive contains
much valuable religious material. For example, I have found "God's
Word for Windows" to be very useful.

This policy statement also seems to be claiming that the U. of Indiana
prohibits "prejudiced or discriminatory" messages in archives. I don't
believe that this is true. Such a prohibition would be illegal
viewpoint-based discrimination. (See refs for much discussion on
viewpoint-based discrimination).

If *you-the-archivist* want to prohibit material because of partisan
or doctrinal disapproval, just say so. Don't try to rationalize your
policy with implicit lies about university policy. Such
rationalizations just add insult to injury.

- Carl

ANNOTATED REFERENCES

(All these documents are available on-line. Access information follows.)

=================
law/san-diego-committee-v-gov-bd
=================
* Expression -- Public Forum -- Overview -- San Diego Committee v. Gov Bd

Excerpts from San Diego Committee v.  Governing Bd., 790 F.2d 1471.  A
decision by an appellate court that applied the Supreme Court's Public
Forum Doctrine (to a school newspaper).

=================
law/cornelius-v-naacp
=================
* Expression -- Restrictions w. Disguised Purpose -- Cornelius v. NAACP

In Cornelius v. NAACP Legal Defense and Education Fund (1985), the SC
ruled "The existence of reasonable grounds for limiting access to a
nonpublic forum, however, will not save a regulation that is in
reality a facade for viewpoint-based discrimination..."


=================
law/iskcon-v-lee
=================
* Expression -- Public Forum -- Airports -- Iskcon v. Lee

Supreme Court orders, summaries, and opinions for case 91-155 (_ISKCON
v. Lee_) and 91-339 (_Lee v. ISKCON_).

ISKCON is the International Society for Krishna Consciousness.

The Court ruled that neither by tradition nor purpose can the airport
terminals be decribed as public fora (forums). The Port Authority
airports are thus nonpublic fora, and thus "reasonable" restrictions,
such as a ban on solicitation, are Constitutional. A ban on literature
distribution, however, is unreasonable.

=================
law/moderator.rights
=================
* Expression -- Moderator Rights

A summary of the rights of an editor/moderator. Addresses the
question: "if a moderator is government-site-based, what is his or her
authority/right to exclude material?"

=================
law/rav-v-st-paul.1
=================
* Expression -- Hate Speech -- RAV v. St Paul -- 1

The Supreme Court's _R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul_ decision about hate crimes.

The Court overturned St. Paul's Bias-Motivated Crime Ordinance, which
prohibits the display of a symbol which one knows or has reason to
know "arouses anger, alarm or resentment in others on the basis of
race, color, creed, religion or gender."

By 9-0, the Court said the law as overly broad. By 5-4, the Court said
that the law was also unfairly selective because it only tried to protect
some groups.

Included: summary, majority opinion, 3 concurring opinions.

=================
law/rust-v-sullivan
=================
* Expression -- Gag Rule -- Rust v. Sullivan

The decision and decent for the so-called abortion information gag
rule case. The decision explicitly mentions universities as a place
where free expression is so important that gag rules would not be
allowed.

